he \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${x}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=4x-2xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the $\\text{prey}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $4x$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-2xy$ term)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16481b80-9744-5c8d-843a-14af30fd8cf4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying and Interpreting the Lotka-Volterra Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c9479d2-cbfb-5345-96fe-5db52a2bfdc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "rate of change" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370172} {:answer/id #uuid "928a10d4-85a1-58a7-996c-2b7a024d0c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "amount" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c9479d2-cbfb-5345-96fe-5db52a2bfdc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "rate of change" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370172} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e658e15-e093-5ab8-b271-2c0a1b0834fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370171} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1973650f-7466-5fac-be98-b91c9a6bb3b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "predator" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370175} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f7a61f-1667-541b-b5eb-eab40fabf669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prey" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1f7a61f-1667-541b-b5eb-eab40fabf669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prey" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370176} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e763f25-55a8-5e05-b176-9a86fa5cdf1a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ccaa6cb-56f1-5809-80e7-633a3a4dc02a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334985" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2.5x-0.5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+0.8xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${x}^{′}$ in the first equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${x}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=2.5x-0.5xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the $\\text{prey}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $2.5x$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-0.5xy$ term)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16481b80-9744-5c8d-843a-14af30fd8cf4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying and Interpreting the Lotka-Volterra Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e4899d3-ec39-5328-b6b4-ec29e3ec2902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "rate of change" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445030} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1b4bdf-e9c1-5d5b-acee-d33c7f800149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "amount" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e4899d3-ec39-5328-b6b4-ec29e3ec2902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "rate of change" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445030} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bdcfa39-d426-5f32-aadc-e305d4d7db21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445087} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c98e75b-1b50-5f02-974a-29c12fa13e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "predator" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445041} {:answer/id #uuid "aa59b7af-054a-5639-bbba-7a80a086495c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prey" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c98e75b-1b50-5f02-974a-29c12fa13e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "predator" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445041} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62a6bf9-30b3-5c9e-ab14-91175aa58de8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c2a30e4-7147-5605-9ca8-d3892b9005ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-334986" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2.5x+3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=4.5y-1.5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${x}^{′}$ in the first equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${x}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=-2.5x+3xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the $\\text{predator}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-2.5x$ term), and\n\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $3xy$ term)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16481b80-9744-5c8d-843a-14af30fd8cf4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying and Interpreting the Lotka-Volterra Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10bc4a5f-cfa8-5e4e-a6dd-8a34056a0b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "rate of change" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444993} {:answer/id #uuid "32668252-978b-5bc2-9826-63d40da6593b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "amount" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10bc4a5f-cfa8-5e4e-a6dd-8a34056a0b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "rate of change" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444993} :answer-field/id #uuid "0385b0de-5182-56de-87fe-bd21709d14e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445076} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b5ac13a-424b-5aca-8e68-5c39590183ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "predator" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445036} {:answer/id #uuid "919fe6e3-cdeb-566f-be07-fcc52bc70af2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prey" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b5ac13a-424b-5aca-8e68-5c39590183ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "predator" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445036} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a3e6e3a-0204-57e7-bf52-d34b7b48737e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6c858ce-3897-5421-9543-5d24a7c86dfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-334991" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=6x-0.9xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3.2y+2.4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${y}^{′}$ in the second equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${y}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3.2y+2.4xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$ must represent the $\\text{predator}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-3.2y$ term), and\n\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $2.4xy$ term)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f77fc78-6c6b-5dd0-ac2b-7822e4cb961f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fab5b32-10a1-5a46-94ca-9eb01b58c24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fab5b32-10a1-5a46-94ca-9eb01b58c24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370178} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d434ee-ec78-51ed-8e2a-b9f813737294" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370177} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb1a7389-5d16-5f46-9650-c5051a6920de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb1a7389-5d16-5f46-9650-c5051a6920de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370180} :answer-field/id #uuid "874b57c5-019e-572e-93ae-81d874074f37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0f816f5-6732-572e-beb4-ba284ca82c1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334992" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-2xy=0 \\\\ -4y+5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2xy & =0 \\\\ x(3-2y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{3}{2},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+5xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(-4+5x) & =0 \\\\ -4+5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f77fc78-6c6b-5dd0-ac2b-7822e4cb961f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "223bb1ef-6abd-5b8f-9cc4-9705282c9116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "223bb1ef-6abd-5b8f-9cc4-9705282c9116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444999} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1ce7209-c7d2-5426-8c47-a6db5339bfa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445091} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4042d52e-23f4-5dc8-b1f7-bbe39de58e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4042d52e-23f4-5dc8-b1f7-bbe39de58e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445007} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8c8fb6d-38bf-50ae-8c71-d42b9e1d88c2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "deb74bf3-ea72-561b-8b82-34ede161ce61" :question/math-academy-id "q-334996" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+7xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=6y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy=0 \\\\ 6y-5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy & =0 \\\\ x(-4+7y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{4}{7}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y-5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{4}{7},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y-5xy & =0 \\\\ y(6-5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}(6-5x) & =0 \\\\ 6-5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{6}{5},\\frac{4}{7}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{6}{5},\\frac{4}{7}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f77fc78-6c6b-5dd0-ac2b-7822e4cb961f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "895380dc-30e3-5fd2-bca3-c6f6c57018ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "895380dc-30e3-5fd2-bca3-c6f6c57018ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370193} :answer-field/id #uuid "d04708cb-1115-59d0-a2c4-ebbfdb85401a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370192} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc10c4dd-0f2c-55a3-bde8-eae31ae0d2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc10c4dd-0f2c-55a3-bde8-eae31ae0d2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370195} :answer-field/id #uuid "5acf4717-4ac3-5126-9b5d-7a7393e636d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ecc92fa2-9bc0-55ee-9c55-3ca595f577e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-335000" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-5x+4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=9y-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x+4xy=0 \\\\ 9y-2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+4xy & =0 \\\\ x(-5+4y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{5}{4}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-2(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{5}{4},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-2xy & =0 \\\\ y(9-2x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}(9-2x) & =0 \\\\ 9-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f77fc78-6c6b-5dd0-ac2b-7822e4cb961f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f05771b2-5acd-5c74-bcf1-b8647b5a5a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f05771b2-5acd-5c74-bcf1-b8647b5a5a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445072} :answer-field/id #uuid "aebfc45b-de88-5aad-aeaf-288e839a09f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445093} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2759ddb-1657-5331-8039-4a80dc4d88e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2759ddb-1657-5331-8039-4a80dc4d88e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445059} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc175313-c1cd-5ef5-81ac-641814b985cd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54015625-9f74-5e9e-ac7d-555ab045f6d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335003" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=7x-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7x-4xy=0 \\\\ -6y+3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-4xy & =0 \\\\ x(7-4y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{7}{4}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{7}{4},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+3x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}(-6+3x) & =0 \\\\ -6+3x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(2,\\frac{7}{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f77fc78-6c6b-5dd0-ac2b-7822e4cb961f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of the Lotka-Volterra System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "401db806-372a-5fd6-8992-621c15114775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "401db806-372a-5fd6-8992-621c15114775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370198} :answer-field/id #uuid "d51c4e9d-4fc3-5b94-9fbb-2f1729282196" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370197} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e1d1667-541b-5035-a21b-fbdba15ef778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e1d1667-541b-5035-a21b-fbdba15ef778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370200} :answer-field/id #uuid "afe78aeb-3059-5e8a-9a99-9dcbe521ca11" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0c7e73b-2cb7-5aa8-bc2d-519f7e72ef51" :question/math-academy-id "q-335006" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2x+5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=7y-3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+5xy=0 \\\\ 7y-3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+5xy & =0 \\\\ x(-2+5y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-3(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{2}{5},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-3xy & =0 \\\\ y(7-3x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(7-3x) & =0 \\\\ 7-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{2}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{2}{5}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1fa1647-1acb-50bd-95e7-26ed0c495bd4" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Lotka-Volterra Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dbff882-e5e6-5756-a325-fd54b19a41e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an asymptotically stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445022} {:answer/id #uuid "810c1ce2-2836-5f5b-8d80-bf8c755c3ea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445037} {:answer/id #uuid "dab3322e-8c73-5c38-ac04-a9b67a39acfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dab3322e-8c73-5c38-ac04-a9b67a39acfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445065} :answer-field/id #uuid "1431c518-0720-5741-b2e4-d9808709c654" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ba76fe6-3b8b-54f7-93d0-0febd41e8f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218644