{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445002} {:answer/id #uuid "52af2432-7832-5b6a-b95e-64eb7b7f7851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445016} {:answer/id #uuid "841585f7-14a9-5f6f-bb0e-f8ee3a4d16c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445039} {:answer/id #uuid "d56e4dbb-11c4-5160-acd1-ca64933cd59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445063} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4e2c78-5978-54ed-929b-5b4a23a789f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d56e4dbb-11c4-5160-acd1-ca64933cd59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445063} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0cdbe5f-be3e-576b-a6f3-c613a26e6a75" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3850fcb7-fc55-55b2-8f9f-750fe16ba10c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335012" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=0.8x-0.4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-0.3y+1.2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.25,\\,2]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is\n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0.8x-0.4xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-0.3y+1.2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0.8x-0.4xy)=0.8-0.4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0.8x-0.4xy)=-0.4x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.3y+1.2xy)=1.2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.3y+1.2xy)=-0.3+1.2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}0.8-0.4y & -0.4x \\\\ 1.2y & -0.3+1.2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}0.8-0.4(2) & -0.4(0.25) \\\\ 1.2(2) & -0.3+1.2(0.25)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -0.1 \\\\ 2.4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -0.1 \\\\ 2.4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-2.4\\cdot (-0.1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 0.2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is $\\text{a stable}$ $\\text{center}.$\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c9c2220-030e-5418-915c-e34d7962c077" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing a Solution of the Lotka-Volterra Model Given the Phase Portrait"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5a2af56-80f8-51d0-81e6-386c6d2e4869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370281} {:answer/id #uuid "42f15a47-847b-52f6-a792-7f19e2f65c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42f15a47-847b-52f6-a792-7f19e2f65c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increases" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370282} :answer-field/id #uuid "fccf4520-0225-5f46-879f-c5c1d6a6f1be" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370280} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fff24be-bf5e-59e2-8ea3-c5927b4c96f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "circles around" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370284} {:answer/id #uuid "8096924d-ffa3-555c-b093-72ab123510cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "approaches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fff24be-bf5e-59e2-8ea3-c5927b4c96f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "circles around" :db/